findings.body:
The flush wrapper saves `orig_active_defrag`, sets the flag to 0, and then restores it to `1`. The saved value is never read. A replica configured with `activedefrag no` will have defrag turned on after its first full sync, which the operator did not ask for. The restore should write back the saved value.

::diff{file="src/replication.c" lines="1949-1965"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The unused `orig_active_defrag` also triggers `-Wunused-but-set-variable` under the `-Wall` flags in src/Makefile.

::suggestion{file="src/replication.c" lines="1961-1962"}
    /* Restore the original active defragmentation setting. */
    server.active_defrag_enabled = orig_active_defrag;

`streamEntryIsReferenced()` returns 0 when no entry is in any PEL, which is the correct answer for `ACKED` trimming. The new guard `if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 1;` returns 1 instead. `cgroups_ref` is NULL whenever no PEL entries exist, for example after a `DEBUG RELOAD` with an empty PEL, which is exactly the case the new test describes. Once that happens, every entry that a group has already delivered and acknowledged is treated as referenced. `XADD ... MAXLEN ... ACKED` and `XTRIM ... ACKED` then stop removing those entries, which silently changes trimming behavior.

The new test in tests/unit/type/stream.tcl expects `XLEN` to be 2 after the last `ACKED` add. Tracing it: after the second reload the PEL is empty, so `cgroups_ref` is NULL. When `1-0` is trimmed, it passes the `min_cgroup_last_id` check, reaches the guard, and returns 1, so it is kept. `XLEN` becomes 3, and the assertion fails.

The right value is 0: a NULL `cgroups_ref` means no PEL holds the entry.

::diff{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2704-2711"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The crash fix is correct in intent. The return value is the bug.

::suggestion{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2708-2708"}
    if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 0;

The new helper drains replies only when `$count % $frequency != 0`, which is the opposite of the original `if {$count % 10000 == 0}` logic it replaces. It reads 10000 replies on every iteration except every 10000th, while the client has only sent one command per iteration. The deferring client's `read` will block waiting for replies that never arrive. Every call site that uses this helper (the existing defrag tests and the new flush test) will hang, and the replies that do exist will not be drained on the right boundary.

::diff{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="70-76"}

::suggestion{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="71-71"}
        if {$count % $frequency == 0} {

The new test wraps the whole scenario in `if {[$replica config get activedefrag] eq "activedefrag yes"}`. If `config set activedefrag yes` fails (for example on a non-jemalloc build), the `catch` swallows the error and the test passes without asserting anything. The existing tests in the same file gate on `string match {*jemalloc*} [s mem_allocator]` and `r debug mallctl arenas.page` in `test_active_defrag`. This one does not, so it never fails in that case.

The test also never verifies the flush-time behavior directly. The `wait_for_defrag_stop` and `dbsize == 0` checks run only inside that branch.

